  "worked_solution": "The curvature $\u03ba(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\n\u03ba(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{\u2032}(t){y}^{\u2033}(t)-{y}^{\u2032}(t){x}^{\u2033}(t)|}{([{x}^{\u2032}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{\u2032}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{\u03ba(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t),\\,y(t)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t),\\, & {y}^{\u2032}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t) \\\\ {x}^{\u2033}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t),\\, & {y}^{\u2033}(t) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u03ba(t) & =\\frac{|2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t))-(-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t))|}{{((2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2t))^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-8{cos}^{2}\u2061(2t)-8{sin}^{2}\u2061(2t)|}{{(4{cos}^{2}\u2061(2t)+4{sin}^{2}\u2061(2t))}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u03ba(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{\u03ba(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
